Overlay patch 0067 (D-086): ASYNCHRONOUS DEPTH READBACK (sun lens flare + light glows).

WHY. Round 2's probe (0066) on the user's iPhone Air (build 202610090020, HD
pack, 120 Hz, thermal 0) put the game-thread spikes on one seam: zdep, the
commit + waitUntilCompleted inside GfxRenderingAPIMetal::GetPixelDepth. It is
called once per game tick by Environment_GraphCallback (z_kankyo.c) for the
sun's lens-flare occlusion test and the point-light glows (z_lights.c).
A fresh command buffer on the frame queue runs behind every frame already
queued, so the wait is the GPU's whole backlog: 3-5 ms per tick in the better
windows, 10-15 ms per tick in the bad ones (1.9-2.9 s of every 10 s). Every
logged spike was a tick's first frame with logic 18-20 ms, of it zdep 16-19.

HOW THE ORIGINAL DID IT. graph.c Graph_TaskSet00 runs gfxCtx->callback right
after osRecvMesg(&gfxCtx->queue) returns, i.e. after the PREVIOUS frame's
RDP task finished and before this frame's task is sent. On N64,
Environment_GetPixelDepth read gZBuffer directly, so the callback read the
z-buffer of the previous frame at the coordinates this frame's draw just
computed, and the results (D_8011FB44, LightPoint.drawGlow) are consumed by
the NEXT frame's draw. SoH keeps that order: the callback reads the last
frame already submitted (tick N-1's), synchronously.

WHAT THIS PATCH DOES (gSohIos.AsyncDepth, default 1; 0 = upstream sync path).
Two phases per tick, no CPU wait anywhere:
  1. SUBMIT, in Environment_GraphCallback (end of tick N): the depth-sample
     compute dispatch for this tick's coordinates is encoded into its own
     command buffer and committed, NOT waited on. Queue order puts it right
     after tick N-1's frames and before tick N's, so it samples exactly the
     depth the synchronous path would have read. The values returned now are
     provisional (newest completed result) -- nothing reads them before (2).
  2. HARVEST, at the top of Play_Main (start of tick N+1, before Play_Update
     and Play_Draw, the only readers): the callback's body runs again. No game
     state has changed since (1), so it asks for the same coordinates, and
     the backend answers them from the dispatch of (1), which finished on the
     GPU during the ~50 ms tick. D_8011FB44 and drawGlow are then the values
     the synchronous path would have produced -- same pixels, same frame, no
     added latency for the consumers.
Fallbacks: if (1)'s dispatch has not finished (GPU more than one tick
behind), the nearest coordinate of the newest finished dispatch within
height/16 px is used (the previous tick's view of that light); farther
than that, 0xFFFC (far: not occluded). If no dispatch has ever finished
(first tick after launch), the harvest runs the upstream synchronous path
once. Four result slots (own coord + output buffers each), oldest finished
one reused; none free = skip that submit (counted). The interpreter's
per-frame depth cache is dropped once at the start of each pass: otherwise a
harvest could be answered with the submit pass's provisional values, and --
found on the sim, first build -- a submit pass with unchanged coordinates
(still camera) was answered from the harvest's cache, never reached the GPU,
and the samples went stale.

VERIFICATION MODE (gSohIos.AsyncDepthCheck, default 0): the submit pass also
runs upstream's synchronous readback for the same coordinates; the harvest
compares every exact answer with it (SOH_ZASYNC check_eq / check_ne). It
costs the old GPU wait, so it is for the sim / a bench only.

COUNTERS. SOH_ZASYNC every 10 s: submit, skip (no free slot), exact, near,
far, sync (first-time fallback), inflight (slots still on the GPU at harvest).
The 0066 zdep seam still wraps the whole call, so zdep now measures the
encode + commit cost (both phases count, n ~= 400 per 10 s at 20 Hz).

Platform: __IOS__ is defined for the iPhone and visionOS builds (overlay
0022 re-flips visionOS to iOS for LUS), so both get it; the macOS build is
unchanged.

Generate against a vendor tree with 0001..0066 applied and 0067 not applied.


